      <!-- SECTION 7 -->
      <section class="section faq-section" id="faq">
        <h2>7. Frequently Asked Questions (FAQ)</h2>
        <p>
          The following technical questions surface repeatedly from startups and industrial procurement teams evaluating enzyme-based cleaning systems. Each answer addresses the underlying chemistry and engineering reality rather than marketing surface, so they can also be reused inside your own technical bulletins and customer training material.
        </p>

        <div style="display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 20px; margin-top: 20px;">

          <div class="box" style="margin: 0; background: #ffffff; border: 1px solid var(--border); padding: 20px; border-radius: var(--radius-md);">
            <h3 style="color: var(--primary); font-size: 1.15rem; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px;">1. Are enzyme cleaners safe for children and pets?</h3>
            <p style="margin: 0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 0.95rem;">
              Most enzyme cleaners are formulated near skin-neutral pH (6.5–8.5) and use biodegradable surfactant systems, making them substantially safer than chlorine bleach or quaternary ammonium products. However, the safety profile is determined by the entire system — preservatives, fragrances, and secondary surfactants must also be evaluated under regional cosmetics and biocide regulations.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="box" style="margin: 0; background: #ffffff; border: 1px solid var(--border); padding: 20px; border-radius: var(--radius-md);">
            <h3 style="color: var(--primary); font-size: 1.15rem; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px;">2. How long does it take for an enzyme cleaner to work?</h3>
            <p style="margin: 0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 0.95rem;">
              Enzymes follow Michaelis-Menten kinetics, so action begins on contact but visible results require dwell time. For drain biofilms or organic odor sources, 15 minutes to several hours of wet contact at the enzyme's optimal temperature is needed for full hydrolysis of proteins, lipids, and polysaccharides.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="box" style="margin: 0; background: #ffffff; border: 1px solid var(--border); padding: 20px; border-radius: var(--radius-md);">
            <h3 style="color: var(--primary); font-size: 1.15rem; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px;">3. Do enzyme cleaners expire?</h3>
            <p style="margin: 0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 0.95rem;">
              Yes. Enzymes are tertiary-folded proteins and progressively denature over time, especially in the presence of incompatible surfactants, oxidizers, or extreme pH. A correctly stabilized formulation using polyols (sorbitol, glycerin), calcium ion bridging, and balanced preservative chemistry can hold 70–90% of activity for 18–24 months at ambient storage.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="box" style="margin: 0; background: #ffffff; border: 1px solid var(--border); padding: 20px; border-radius: var(--radius-md);">
            <h3 style="color: var(--primary); font-size: 1.15rem; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px;">4. Can bleach be combined with enzyme cleaners?</h3>
            <p style="margin: 0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 0.95rem;">
              No. Sodium hypochlorite is a strong oxidizer that irreversibly oxidizes the disulfide bridges and key methionine residues in enzymes, destroying their catalytic active sites within seconds. The two systems must be marketed and stored as completely separate product lines.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="box" style="margin: 0; background: #ffffff; border: 1px solid var(--border); padding: 20px; border-radius: var(--radius-md);">
            <h3 style="color: var(--primary); font-size: 1.15rem; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px;">5. What temperature is best for enzyme activity?</h3>
            <p style="margin: 0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 0.95rem;">
              Conventional bacterial proteases and lipases peak between 30°C and 60°C, with steep deactivation above 65°C due to thermal unfolding. Cold-active variants engineered from psychrophilic microorganisms remain effective from 5°C upward, while thermostable enzymes derived from Bacillus species tolerate up to 90°C for industrial CIP cycles.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="box" style="margin: 0; background: #ffffff; border: 1px solid var(--border); padding: 20px; border-radius: var(--radius-md);">
            <h3 style="color: var(--primary); font-size: 1.15rem; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px;">6. How can a startup compete in the enzyme cleaner market?</h3>
            <p style="margin: 0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 0.95rem;">
              Differentiation comes from technical niches that large detergent multinationals underserve — hospital-grade enzymatic instrument cleaners, septic and grease-trap maintenance blocks, pet enzymatic odor neutralizers, or food-safe CIP foam cleaners. Partnering with a formulation consultancy that controls enzyme stability and substrate-specific blends is what enables a startup-scale product to match big-brand performance.
            </p>
          </div>

        </div>
      </section>

